# Line endings are repo state, not developer configuration.
#
# Without this file, normalization depends on each clone's `core.autocrlf`. On
# this machine it happens to be `input`, which is why the index is already all
# LF - but `package.json` and `package-lock.json` show as `i/lf w/crlf` in the
# working tree (npm rewrites them with CRLF on Windows), so the divergence is
# live rather than hypothetical. A contributor with the Windows default
# (`core.autocrlf=true`, or unset with Git for Windows' installer default)
# would commit CRLF and produce whole-file diffs.
#
# `text eol=lf` normalizes to LF in the index *and* checks out LF, which is what
# every consumer here wants: Node/Vite/ESLint/Prettier tooling, the bash `run:`
# blocks in .github/workflows (CRLF there breaks `set -euo pipefail` scripts on
# ubuntu-latest), and the GLSL shader sources.
#
# Verify with: git ls-files --eol
* text eol=lf

# Binary files must never be EOL-converted. The committed PNGs are review
# evidence regenerated by `npm run browser:verify`; byte-mangling them would
# corrupt the images and produce meaningless diffs. `binary` is Git's shorthand
# for `-text -diff`, so these are also excluded from textual diffing.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.webp binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Explicit entries for the three artifacts actually committed today, so the
# intent survives even if the glob above is ever narrowed.
artifacts/browser-desktop.png binary
artifacts/browser-mobile.png binary
artifacts/browser-gltf.png binary

# glTF assets: .gltf is JSON (text), .glb is a binary container.
*.gltf text eol=lf
*.glb binary

# Lockfile is generated; keep it LF-normalized but out of diff review noise.
package-lock.json text eol=lf -diff linguist-generated
